## DESCRIPTION
## Calculate the batting averages.
## ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Arithmetic)
## DBchapter(Percents)
## DBsection(Applications)
## KEYWORDS('fraction', 'percent','batting average');
## Level(1)
## MO(1)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'randomPerson.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

@p = randomPerson(n => 3);

$hit1 = random(12,  18);
$hit2 = random(109, 159, 10);
$ab2  = random(451, 501, 10);
$hit3 = random(36,  45);
$ab3  = random(140, 150);
$ba3  = Compute(0.001 * round(1000 * $hit3 / $ab3));

$ans1 = Compute($hit1 / 50);
$ans2 = Compute(0.001 * round(1000 * $hit2 / $ab2));
$ans3 = Compute(100 * $ans2);
$ans4 = Compute($hit3);

BEGIN_PGML
Baseball batting averages are somewhat similar to percentages except that they
are usually rounded to three decimal places and include the decimal point.  That is, if a player got 3 hits in 10 at bats then she had a hit [`\frac {3}{10} = .300`] of the times she was at bat. Her batting average is listed as .300 in the newspapers.

a) If [$p[0]] got [$hit1] hits in 50 times at bat, [$p[0]->their] batting average is [_]{$ans1}.

b) If [$p[1]] got [$hit2] hits in [$ab2] times at bat, [$p[1]->their] batting average is [_]{$ans2}.

c) If [$p[1]] got [$hit2] hits in [$ab2] times at bat, then [$p[1]->they] got hits 
    [_]{$ans3->cmp(tolerance => 0.05, tolType => 'absolute')}% of the times [$p[1]->they] [$p[1]->were] at bat. 
    
    _Answer should be correct to 1 decimal place._

d) [$p[2]]'s batting average is [$ba3].  [$p[2]->They] [$p[2]->were] at bat [$ab3] times.  That means [$p[2]->they] got [_]{$ans4} hits.
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
In a) just divide [$hit1] by 50 and round to three decimal places.  
Answer is [$ans1].

In b) divide [$hit2] by [$ab2] and round to three decimal places.  
Answer is [$ans2]. 

In c) Your answer should be a percentage.  So multiply the decimal
batting average in b) by 100.  
Answer is [$ans3].

In d) since batting average is hits/(at bats), hits must be batting
average times at bats, so multiply [$ba3] by at bats and round the
answer to be an integer. 

You must round because the batting average is approximate since it is only calculated to 3 decimal places.  
Ans = [$ans4].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
